WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.438 --> 00:00:03.727
Unit twenty. Binary Numbers!

2
00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:09.285
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emily, your technology teacher.

3
00:00:09.335 --> 00:00:12.496
Hi, Ms. Emily! What do we learn today?

4
00:00:12.546 --> 00:00:16.242
Today we learn about Binary Numbers. Let's go!

5
00:00:17.822 --> 00:00:21.780
Binary. Say it with me. Binary!

6
00:00:25.316 --> 00:00:28.916
Zero. Say it with me. Zero!

7
00:00:32.376 --> 00:00:35.893
One. Say it with me. One!

8
00:00:39.386 --> 00:00:42.717
Bit. Say it with me. Bit!

9
00:00:46.242 --> 00:00:49.736
Byte. Say it with me. Byte!

10
00:00:53.191 --> 00:00:56.813
Number. Say it with me. Number!

11
00:01:00.334 --> 00:01:04.367
Computer. Say it with me. Computer!

12
00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:11.820
Code. Say it with me. Code!

13
00:01:15.333 --> 00:01:28.263
Computers store everything as binary, using only zero and one. Each zero or one is a bit, and 8 bits make a byte. Letters, pictures, and numbers are all turned into this code.

14
00:01:28.313 --> 00:01:32.183
Now I understand. Thank you, Ms. Emily!

15
00:01:33.195 --> 00:01:38.646
Look! What's the word?

16
00:01:38.696 --> 00:01:39.846
One!

17
00:01:40.664 --> 00:01:46.204
Look! What's the word?

18
00:01:46.254 --> 00:01:47.404
Computer!

19
00:01:48.447 --> 00:01:55.157
Did you know? One byte (8 bits) can store 256 different values!

20
00:01:55.207 --> 00:01:58.547
Wow! I did not know that!

21
00:01:59.528 --> 00:02:08.298
Think and answer. Why do computers use only zeros and ones?

22
00:02:08.348 --> 00:02:09.562
Great answer!

23
00:02:10.427 --> 00:02:16.103
Great job today! Keep learning, and see you next time. Goodbye!

24
00:02:16.153 --> 00:02:19.199
Goodbye, everyone! See you next time!

